#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cairn deletegate — the PreToolUse gate on unrecoverable deletes.

THE MEASURED FAILURE, 2026-08-18. CC ran ``rm -rf ~/.cairn/logs`` to clear eight
diagnostics trails a proof run had seeded, and destroyed six files it had never read —
among them a 54-record pre-image somebody had deliberately archived. Measured within the
minute: ext4, no snapshots, ``~/.cairn`` in no backup. Nothing was recoverable, and the
whole event took one tool call.

WHY A GATE AND NOT A RESOLUTION TO BE CAREFUL. The intent was correct at every step —
clean up after the proofs, using the same verb everybody uses. What went wrong was the
BLAST RADIUS of one flag against one root, and no amount of resolving to be careful
survives the next tidy-up six weeks from now. Law 4: a rule that matters is enforced by
physics, not policy. The policy version of this rule already existed, in CLAUDE.md's
three-roots table, which has said since the beginning that instance-space is never in
git — and it did not stop anything, because a table is read by a mind and a mind is
exactly the component that was going fast.

WHAT IT REFUSES, and it is deliberately one rule with no exemption roster: **a delete
under ~/.cairn goes through bin/cmd/trash.** Not "an unread delete", not "a recursive
delete", not "a delete of more than N files" — every one of those needs someone to
adjudicate an edge at the moment they are least equipped to, and the exemption roster is
what let a node through with no watch at all (``tester/scratch.py`` records that lesson
about this very repo). One root, one verb, one door.

WHAT IT DOES NOT TOUCH, and the bound is the point. The two git roots are NOT gated: a
bad delete there costs a checkout, so gating them would be friction bought with nothing.
That is the same asymmetry the three-roots table already draws; this gate is that table
made physical at the one row where it bites. Untracked files in the git roots are the
honest hole — they are as unrecoverable as instance-space and this does not cover them.
Named rather than closed, because closing it means gating every delete in the working
tree, and n=1 does not buy that much friction (CP4: the rule serves us).

THE REFUSAL IS LOUD AND CARRIES THE WAY THROUGH (Law 7, and complete-diagnostic-on-first-
pass): it names the command that was blocked, why this root is different, and the exact
line to type instead. A gate that only says no gets routed around by the next mind in a
hurry, which is the same mind that needed the gate.

IT MATCHES BY CO-OCCURRENCE, NOT BY CLAUSE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE — the finding, measured
n=2 on 2026-08-18, the day after it shipped. ``verdict`` asks two independent questions of
the command TEXT: is there a destructive verb anywhere, and is there a guarded form
anywhere. It never asks whether they are in the same clause. Both refusals it produced that
day were false positives of exactly that shape: a multi-line command whose ``rm -rf`` and
``find -delete`` targeted ``/tmp`` paths, while a different line of the same command merely
MENTIONED ``$HOME/.cairn/venv`` as a thing to read. The fix was to split the command, and
the gate was left as it is.

That is the trade taken with its eyes open: a false refuse costs one retyped command, a
false allow costs the data, and there is no symmetric version of this because the two
outcomes are not the same size. Parsing shell into clauses to tighten it would mean
building a shell parser inside a hook, and a hook that reasons about ``$(...)``,
here-documents and ``eval`` has become a thing that can be wrong in ways nobody reviews —
against a cost of one retype. Recorded here so the next mind meeting a puzzling refusal
reads a measured property rather than a bug, and knows the answer is to split the command.

ONE NARROWING, MEASURED (ticket 76d35575aead): the body of a DATA here-document is not read
as a command. Measured 2026-10-08, n>=5 — an append to a ``~/.cairn`` note whose prose said
"rm" was refused by co-occurrence, every time a mind wrote down what a delete had done. A
here-document is data when the segment holding its ``<<`` starts with ``cat`` or ``tee`` and
nothing after the heredoc word on that line pipes onward; its body (through the terminator)
is dropped before the two questions are asked, and the command line itself always stays. A
here-document fed to anything else — ``bash``, ``sh``, ``python3``, a ``cat`` piped onward —
is judged exactly as before, and so is one with no terminator. The trade is unchanged in
kind: a script written by a ``cat`` here-document and run later was never seen by this gate
(a script file's text is not the command), and stripping the body does not widen that.

Contract, measured 2026-08-18 rather than assumed: stdin is JSON carrying ``tool_name``
and ``tool_input.command``; exit 0 allows, exit 2 blocks and feeds stderr back to Claude.
Registered at ``.claude/settings.json`` under ``hooks.PreToolUse`` with matcher ``Bash``.

Proof: ``bin/proofs/test_delete_gate.py`` (exit 0 = green).
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import json
import re
import sys
from pathlib import Path

# The one guarded root. Spelled three ways because a command may carry any of them, and
# the gate reads the TEXT the tool was handed — the shell has not expanded anything yet.
GUARDED = "/.cairn"
GUARDED_FORMS = ("~/.cairn", "$HOME/.cairn", str(Path.home() / ".cairn"), ".cairn")

# The verbs that unlink. `find -delete` and `-exec rm` are here because the miss that
# taught this repo about spellings was exactly this shape: the first corpus tooth for the
# temp-dir leak grepped for `mkdtemp` and missed a leak wearing `gettempdir` (scratch.py,
# 2026-08-03). A gate narrower than its own defect is the hollow kind.
DESTRUCTIVE = re.compile(
    r"(?:^|[\s;&|(])(?:rm|rmdir|shred|unlink)\b"
    r"|(?:^|[\s;&|(])find\b[^;&|]*?(?:-delete\b|-exec\s+rm\b|-execdir\s+rm\b)"
    r"|(?:^|[\s;&|(])truncate\b"
    r"|(?:^|[\s;&|(])(?:mkfs|dd)\b",
)

REFUSAL = """REFUSED — this deletes under ~/.cairn, the one root with no undo.

  blocked: {command}

~/.cairn is instance-space: never in git, on ext4 with no snapshots, in no backup.
A delete here is final. The two git roots are not gated — a bad delete there costs a
checkout — so this refusal is specifically about the row of CLAUDE.md's three-roots
table where the loss is total.

Use the door instead, which is recoverable and shows up in KDE's trash:

  bin/cmd/trash <path>...        move it, recoverably
  bin/cmd/trash --list           see what is in there and where it came from
  bin/cmd/trash --restore <name> put it back

Deleting many files by pattern:

  find ~/.cairn/logs -name diagnostics.jsonl -exec bin/cmd/trash {{}} +

This gate was built the day `rm -rf ~/.cairn/logs` destroyed six files nobody had read,
including a 54-record archive that was kept on purpose. If it is genuinely wrong here,
that is a finding worth recording rather than a flag worth adding — say so to Akien."""


_HEREDOC = re.compile(r"(?<!<)<<(?!<)(-?)\s*(['\"]?)(\w+)\2")
_DATA_WRITERS = ("cat", "tee")


def _strip_data_heredocs(command: str) -> str:
    """The command with every data here-document's body removed (76d35575aead).

    A heredoc is data when the first word of the segment holding its ``<<`` (the text before
    it, after the last ``;`` ``&`` ``|`` or ``(`` on that line) is ``cat`` or ``tee`` and the
    rest of that line carries no ``|``. Its body lines through the terminator (the line equal
    to the word, leading tabs stripped for ``<<-``) are dropped; with no terminator, nothing
    is. The line carrying the ``<<`` is always kept."""
    lines = command.split("\n")
    out, i = [], 0
    while i < len(lines):
        line = lines[i]
        out.append(line)
        i += 1
        m = _HEREDOC.search(line)
        if not m:
            continue
        segment = re.split(r"[;&|(]", line[:m.start()])[-1].split()
        if not segment or segment[0] not in _DATA_WRITERS or "|" in line[m.end():]:
            continue
        word, tabs = m.group(3), m.group(1) == "-"
        end = next((j for j in range(i, len(lines))
                    if (lines[j].lstrip("\t") if tabs else lines[j]) == word), None)
        if end is not None:
            i = end + 1
    return "\n".join(out)


def verdict(command: str) -> str | None:
    """``None`` to allow; the refusal text to block. The whole judgement, one function,
    so the proof measures what the hook does rather than something shaped like it."""
    if not command:
        return None
    original, command = command, _strip_data_heredocs(command)
    if not DESTRUCTIVE.search(command):
        return None
    if not any(form in command for form in GUARDED_FORMS):
        return None
    # The door itself must pass — otherwise the way through is also blocked, and the gate
    # is a wall. Read as a substring of the command because that is how it will be typed.
    if "cmd/trash" in command or "bin/cmd/trash" in command:
        return None
    return REFUSAL.format(command=original.strip()[:400])


def _main() -> int:
    try:
        payload = json.load(sys.stdin)
    except (json.JSONDecodeError, ValueError):
        # CP1: say what happened. A gate that cannot read its input must not silently
        # allow — but it must not block the whole session either, so it speaks and allows.
        print("deletegate: could not read the hook payload as JSON — allowing, unguarded",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 0

    if payload.get("tool_name") != "Bash":
        return 0

    refusal = verdict((payload.get("tool_input") or {}).get("command", ""))
    if refusal is None:
        return 0
    print(refusal, file=sys.stderr)
    return 2


if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(_main())
